8 true] [17592186092017 1145 "From the given information, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T$. So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1} \\cdot T \\cdot v &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nv &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1} &= (1)/(-15 - (-20))\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534348 false] [17592186092018 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle $S'$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$. The triangle $S'$ has vertices $(4,3),(4,10)$, and $(-4,-3)$. Which of the following are vertices of $S$?\n\n1. $(1,0)$\n2. $(4,-3)$\n3. $(-1,0)$" 13194139534348 false] [17592186092018 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle $S'$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ The triangle $S'$ has vertices $(4,3),(4,10),$ and $(-4,-3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n\n- $(4,-3)$\n\n- $(-1,0)$" 13194139534348 true] [17592186092018 1145 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of $S'$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT \\cdot X &= Y \\\\\nT \\cdot X &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T$. So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1} \\cdot T \\cdot X &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nX &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1} &= (1)/(4 - (-3))\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 28 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(2,4),(-1,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534348 false] [17592186092018 1145 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of $S'\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(2,4),(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534348 true] [17592186092172 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283836 13194139534348 true] [17592186092172 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283843 13194139534348 true] [17592186092172 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283848 13194139534348 true] [17592186092172 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283853 13194139534348 true] [17592186092172 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283859 13194139534348 true] [17592186092173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283864 13194139534348 true] [17592186092173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283871 13194139534348 true] [17592186092173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283878 13194139534348 true] [17592186092173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283885 13194139534348 true] [17592186092843 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534348 true] [17592186092843 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram $P^{′}$ under the action of $T$. The parallelogram $P^{′}$ has vertices $(0, 2), (-3, 3), (12, 4)$, and $(7, 3)$. Which of the following are 